  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe current record for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap constant is 0.3808. We propose to improve upon this by using a multi-granularity optimization approach that combines local search with population-based optimization. This method will:\n\n- Use gradient information to escape local optima\n- Maintain diversity across different scales\n- Employ a hierarchical strategy spanning multiple resolutions\n- Leverage local refinement with continuous optimization\n\nThe central idea is to optimize at various resolution levels simultaneously, allowing both global exploration and fine-scale adaptation. This addresses the limitations of grid-based methods which may miss optimal configurations and the memory constraints of high-resolution searches.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: h is represented as a piecewise constant function on [0,2] over n samples for optimization\n- Objective: Minimize max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx using mode='full' correlation approach\n- Constraints: \u222bh(x)dx=1 and 0\u2264h(x)\u22641 for all x\n- Formulation: We formulate this as a convex-constrained optimization problem that can be solved using projected gradient methods with custom smoothing\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nThe optimization will follow these stages:\n\n- Initialization: Always begin with the provided initial_h_values (n=400) but consider different resolutions for search\n- Resampling: Successively refine solutions to higher resolutions\n- Optimization Rounds:\n  Phase1: Gradient-based optimization with multiple starting points\n  Phase2: Simultaneous search across resolution levels\n  Phase3: Local refinement with continuous relaxation\n\na. Projector:\n```python\ndef project(h, n):\n    target = n / 2.0\n    h = h.copy()\n    lo, hi = -2.0, 2.0\n    for _ in range(100):\n        mid = (lo + hi) / 2\n        hp = np.clip(h - mid, 0.0, 1.0)\n        if abs(hp.sum() - target) < 1e-6:\n            break\n        lo = mid if hp.sum() > target else hi\n    h = np.clip(h - (lo + hi) / 2, 0.0, 1.0)\n    return h\n```\n\nb. Projected Gradient Descent:\n```python\ndef pgd_optimize_init(h, n, n_sweeps=1000):\n    dx = 2.0 / n\n    best_h = h.copy()\n    best_c5 = compute_c5(best_h, n)\n    for _ in range(n_sweeps):\n        if time_remaining < 5: break\n        grad = compute_c5_gradient(best_h, n)\n        accepted = False\n        step = 0.5\n        while step > 1e-7:\n            direction = -grad / np.linalg.norm(grad) if grad is not None else np.ones(n) if np.random.random() < 0.5 else np.zeros(n)\n            h_new = best_h + step * direction\n            h_new = project(h_new, n)\n            c_new = compute_c5(h_new, n)\n            if c_new < best_c5:\n                best_h = h_new.copy()\n                best_c5 = c_new\n                accepted = True\n        h = best_h.copy()\n    return best_h, best_c5\n```\n\n4. Search Choices\na. Resolution Management:\n- Base resolution: 1000 samples\n- Refinement resolution increases multiplicatively by factors of 2\n\nb. Population-Based Strategy:\n- Maintain multiple populations at different resolutions\n- Periodically exchange top performers between scales\n- Use scaled coordinates to preserve problem structure\n\nc. Local Refinement:\n- Apply continuous optimization (projected gradient) to top performers\n- Use bounds scaling for better gradient estimation\n\n5. Compute Budget\n- Total budget: 1100s\n- Phase distribution:\n  - Local search (gradient-based): 40%\n  - Multi-resolution search: 40%\n  - Local refinement: 20%\n- Termination check: Every 30s evaluate diverse strategies\n\n6. Validation\n- Always recompute final solution with highest resolution\n- Verify objective via full correlation approach\n- Check sum(h)=target within tolerance\n- Ensure all values in [0,1]\n- Local refinement: Apply project() before final evaluation",
